   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let g be a function with a continuous second derivative such that \\( g(0) = 1 \\), \\( g(2) = 4 \\), \\( g'(0) = 3 \\), and \\( g'(2) = 5 \\). Find the value of \\( \\int_{0}^{2} x \\, g''(x) \\, dx \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 7
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = x \\) and \\( dv = g''(x) \\, dx \\), so \\( du = dx \\) and \\( v = g'(x) \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then the integral equals \\( \\left[x \\, g'(x)\\right]_{0}^{2} - \\int_{0}^{2} g'(x) \\, dx \\), and the boundary term is \\( 2(5) - 0(3) = 10 \\).",
         "mathjson": 10
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The remaining integral is \\( \\int_{0}^{2} g'(x) \\, dx = g(2) - g(0) = 4 - 1 = 3 \\).",
         "mathjson": 3
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So the value is \\( 10 - \\left(3\\right) = 7 \\).",
         "mathjson": 7
      }
   ],
